---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import css from '../styles/legal.css?raw';
import releases from '../data/releases.json';

/* Page "a propos" : l'entite derriere le projet, et pourquoi il existe.
   Elle sert aussi de source aux formulaires d'annuaires (Product Hunt, G2,
   Crunchbase, Wikidata) qui demandent tous une description de l'editeur.
   La version est lue depuis releases.json pour ne jamais devenir fausse. */
const contactEmail = 'admin@hydraetl.com';
const latest = releases.find((r) => !r.prerelease) ?? releases[0];
const version = latest?.tag?.replace(/^v/, '') ?? '';
const released = latest?.published
  ? new Date(latest.published).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric' })
  : '';
const linkedin = 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/bejaoui-bechir-06b2452b/';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Hydra ETL is an open-source declarative ETL engine maintained by Bechir Bejaoui. Pipelines are YAML manifests, cross-checked before any data is read or written."
>
  <style is:global set:html={css}></style>

  <main class="lg-wrap">
    <h1>About Hydra ETL</h1>
    <p class="lg-lead">
      An open-source ETL engine in which a pipeline is <strong>described, not programmed</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      A job in Hydra ETL is a handful of small YAML files &mdash; sources, transformations, destinations
      &mdash; that can be reviewed in a pull request and checked with one command before any data is touched.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      Most data pipelines start as a script. The script works, then it grows: a flag here, a
      <code>try/except</code> there, a hard-coded path, a credential in a variable. Six months later nobody
      can say what the pipeline does without reading all of it, and nobody dares change it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Hydra ETL takes the opposite route. The pipeline is a declaration.
      <a href="/cli/validate"><code>hdrctl validate</code></a> cross-checks the sources, the transformations
      and the destinations against each other &mdash; column names, types, load modes &mdash; and fails
      <em>before</em> reading or writing anything. What runs in production is the file you reviewed.
    </p>

    <h2>The project</h2>
    <div class="lg-scroll">
      <table class="lg-table">
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>Current version</th><td>{version} ({released}) &mdash; beta &middot; <a href="/changelog">changelog</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th>License</th><td>AGPL-3.0-or-later &middot; <a href="/pricing">commercial license available</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th>Language</th><td>Python 3.9+</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Package</th><td><a href="https://pypi.org/project/hydra-etl/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">hydra-etl on PyPI</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th>Source</th><td><a href="https://github.com/bejaouibechir/Hydra" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/bejaouibechir/Hydra</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th>Maintainer</th><td><a href={linkedin} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bechir Bejaoui</a> &mdash; Tunisia</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="lg-card">
      <p>
        Hydra ETL is maintained by one person. It is an independent open-source project, not a
        venture-funded company. That shapes the roadmap: features land when they are solid, and the engine
        stays something one person can reason about end to end.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>What you can do with it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Declare</strong> jobs and workflows in <a href="/dsl">YAML</a>, with parameters, environments and secrets kept out of the manifests</li>
      <li><strong>Validate</strong> a whole project before it runs, including across files</li>
      <li><strong>Run</strong> it from the <a href="/cli">hdrctl CLI</a>, a REST API, or <a href="/studio">Hydra Studio</a>&rsquo;s visual canvas &mdash; the same jobs, three surfaces</li>
      <li><strong>Orchestrate</strong> with workflows, sub-projects, cron and file triggers, webhooks, retries, timeouts, rollback and fallback workflows</li>
      <li><strong>Scale</strong> by switching a job from pandas to DuckDB with one line</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Try it without installing anything</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/playground">Playground</a> &mdash; edit and validate pipelines in your browser</li>
      <li><a href="https://killercoda.com/hydra-etl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Killercoda labs</a> &mdash; a guided Linux terminal, no account needed</li>
      <li><a href="/academy">Hydra Academy</a> &mdash; video walkthroughs</li>
      <li><a href="/migrate">Migration guides</a> &mdash; coming from Airflow, dbt, Airbyte, Dagster, Kestra, Meltano, Talend, dlt or Flowman</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="lg-card accent">
      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={`mailto:${contactEmail}`}>{contactEmail}</a> &mdash; reply within two business days.
      </p>
      <p>
        For bugs and feature requests,
        <a href="https://github.com/bejaouibechir/Hydra/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.
        For commercial licensing, see <a href="/pricing">Pricing</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="lg-note">
      See also the <a href="/license">license</a>, the <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> and the
      <a href="/terms">Terms of Use</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
